{"meta":{"name":"MedArc Health Sciences","phase":"MedArc conservative authored answer expansion","questions":30068,"examSets":1201,"adjudicatedAnswers":2325,"originalAnswersTrusted":false,"originalAnswersUsedForScoring":false,"copyrightNote":"All source provenance is retained in private audit materials; respect external image licensing.","accuracyNote":"Provisional independent answers; no imported source answers used for this expansion. External expert review pending.","latestVerificationBatch":"2026-10-19 batch 2 (34 newly reviewed)","newProvisionalAnswers":326,"reviewNotice":"New answers are provisional independent reasoning, not clinical validation. Original source keys not consulted."},"questions":[{"id":"q-569217b9d9cf","stem":"What is the gross form and location of the thyroid gland?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f8bb6221470","stem":"Why did the tumor move upward when the patient swallowed?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9c94791a0ceb","stem":"How did the tumor cause breathlessness?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8468c29e2873","stem":"Which lymph nodes should the physician examine for metastases if a malignant tumor is suspected?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2daa734b1b2f","stem":"What structures can be damaged during thyroidectomy if the surgeon is not careful?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82bf45d105b7","stem":"Post-operatively, the surgeon carefully ensured that the patient was speaking properly. Why?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8ecbee985ac2","stem":"Where is the subclavian vein located?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-de5cbf259bbf","stem":"What surface anatomical landmarks are critical for guiding infraclavicular subclavian catheterization?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Face","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9af901849b36","stem":"Describe the relationships of the subclavian vein with other neurovascular structures in the vicinity. The subclavian vein is the most anterior neurovascular structure in the subclavian triangle. Posterior to it at the first rib is the anterior scalene muscle, which separates the vein from the subclavian artery and the inferior trunk of the brachial plexus. Just medial to the first rib, the subclavian vein is joined by the internal jugular vein before proceeding into the thorax to join with the brachiocephalic vein from the other side to form the superior vena cava. On the left side, the thoracic duct empties into the left subclavian vein at its junction with the internal jugular. 6","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["removed_nonquestion_attribution","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-ad1d30b83a01","stem":"Why might a right-sided approach be preferred to a left-sided one for subclavian catheterization?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Neck","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dabe90dc20ba","stem":"What is the course of the facial nerve?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Where does it emerge from the brain? lower border of the pons"},{"label":"B","text":"What foramen does it enter and exit from the skull? internal auditory meatus, stylomastoid foramen"},{"label":"C","text":"Name the five end branches. temporal, zygomatic, buccal, mandibular, cervical"},{"label":"D","text":"What muscles does it innervate? the muscles of facial expression, platysma, stylohyoid, posterior belly of digastric, and stapedius"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-81bc501e7a1a","stem":"What are the clinical signs of Bell's Palsy?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Face","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-abde8fa0d6d1","stem":"Hyperacusis is noted. What is this? What motor branch of the facial nerve is affected? Hyperacusis is increased sound perception. This happens because the stapedius (a muscle of the middle ear) is innervated by a branch of the facial nerve. Normally, stapedius serves to dampen large vibrations of the tympanic membrane resulting from loud noises by contracting and inhibiting movement of the stapes. So, the \"nerve to the stapedius\" is affected.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2bba7562c6c0","stem":"Facial pain or lack of sensation implies that which nerve is affected?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Face","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fbd0ac8ca7ac","stem":"The physician orders lubricating eye-drops to be applied as needed. Why?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-21663e0ed298","stem":"How would you explain the patient's numbness on the right cheek?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c2174152a9f4","stem":"Which facial muscles do you think are responsible for the patient's complaints?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Human 