f the following type of bond between atoms is the weakest?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ionic bond"},{"label":"B","text":"polar bond"},{"label":"C","text":"covalent bond"},{"label":"D","text":"hydrogen bond"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-636ff6c3324f","stem":"Ionic, non-molecular compounds are likely to have which of the following sets of properties?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"high melting point, often soluble in organic liquids, in pure form do not conduct electricity."},{"label":"B","text":"low melting point, no strong odour, soluble in water, electrical conductivity in solution."},{"label":"C","text":"high melting point, electrical conductivity in solution, no strong odour, often soluble in water"},{"label":"D","text":"low melting point, strong odour, soluble in organic liquids, in pure form do not conduct electricity."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-462af7d77f7a","stem":"Which of the following best describes a molecule?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"The simplest structure in an ionic compound."},{"label":"B","text":"One thousandth of a mole."},{"label":"C","text":"The particles of which covalent compounds are composed."},{"label":"D","text":"The smallest particle of an element."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-c11606e059de","stem":"Which of the following is true of metal elements?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The four most common metal elements in the body have the symbols: C, H, O and N."},{"label":"B","text":"They form compounds with non-metals. 48 4 Chemistry for Physiology"},{"label":"C","text":"When they form compounds, they gain electrons."},{"label":"D","text":"In the body, ions of metal atoms have a negative charge."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"4defadac93fefe43bde843f406ce9739799549e9"},{"id":"q-fbeb8770cf0c","stem":"Which of the following symbols represents a chemical element?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"O"},{"label":"B","text":"CO"},{"label":"C","text":"CO2"},{"label":"D","text":"H3O+"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-92833ae043f2","stem":"What is the name given to the particles that make up a covalent compound?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ions"},{"label":"B","text":"molecules"},{"label":"C","text":"anions and cations"},{"label":"D","text":"metal atoms"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-18e7308df5a8","stem":"Compounds may be described by which of the following sentences?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pure substances that contain two or more elements."},{"label":"B","text":"Materials that are composed of particles called molecules."},{"label":"C","text":"Materials composed of more than one part, and the parts may be present in any proportion."},{"label":"D","text":"Substances that appear on the right hand side of the periodic table."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-5aa347e53662","stem":"The symbol for potassium is which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"PO4"},{"label":"B","text":"Po"},{"label":"C","text":"P"},{"label":"D","text":"K"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["answer-leak or footer removed"]},{"id":"q-01016643d4ec","stem":"“Covalent” is the term applied to which of the following bonds?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Those between an ion and all the surrounding oppositely charged ions."},{"label":"B","text":"The bond between an electrolyte and the surrounding water molecules in a solution."},{"label":"C","text":"Those between non-metal atoms."},{"label":"D","text":"Those between atoms on the left hand side of the periodic table."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_no_source_key_bulk_v4","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"3e4336773dc6bac6531ce116d8fed39678d9667d"},{"id":"q-360977cf038a","stem":"Given that one mole is 6 × 1023 particles, how much is a millimole?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"6 × 1020 particles"},{"label":"B","text":"106 mole"},{"label":"C","text":"10-6 mole"},{"label":"D","text":"6 × 10-3 particles"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-a1246b32b80e","stem":"Which of the following is a definition of a molecule? The particle that composes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"covalent compounds"},{"label":"B","text":"non-metal elements"},{"label":"C","text":"electrolytes"},{"label":"D","text":"ionic compounds"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-737845179e62","stem":"What is the smallest particle of a non-metal element known as?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"a molecule"},{"label":"B","text":"an atom"},{"label":"C","text":"an ion"},{"label":"D","text":"a neutron"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-4d8a6b9b211a","stem":"What is the difference between ions and molecules?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"ions have an electrical charge whereas molecules do not."},{"label":"B","text":"ions are from metal elements only whereas molecules contain only non-­ metal elements"},{"label":"C","text":"ions arise from compounds between non-metal elements, whereas mole- cules arise from metal and non-metal elements"},{"label":"D","text":"an ion may be formed from a single atom but molecules always involve more than one atom"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-2066eb9ff263","stem":"Of the four different types of matter listed below which is not an example of an element?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"hydrogen"},{"label":"B","text":"oxygen"},{"label":"C","text":"water"},{"label":"D","text":"gold"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"],"duplicateStemGroup":"8575f6b062394c8f272c3c3333e3cccb1e93e8ef"},{"id":"q-569b087450f6","stem":"A certain pure substance, A, when heated is changed into two quite different pure substances, C and D. Which of the following statements must be true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A is a compound."},{"label":"B","text":"C and D are not elements."},{"label":"C","text":"A, C and D are all compounds."},{"label":"D","text":"C and D are elements."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-48636a7e0cfd","stem":"Which of the following statements about atoms is FALSE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"They are mostly empty space."},{"label":"B","text":"Nearly all their mass is concentrated in the nucleus."},{"label":"C","text":"In a neutral atom protons and electrons are equal in number."},{"label":"D","text":"The nucleus contains equal numbers of protons and neutrons."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-1a4f2de15997","stem":"In which of the following sequences are particles listed in order of increasing size from left to right?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"electron, atom, proton, molecule"},{"label":"B","text":"molecule, atom, proton, electron"},{"label":"C","text":"atom, proton, electron, molecule"},{"label":"D","text":"electron, proton, atom, molecule"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-66eb3be74f68","stem":"Which of the following is the name of a subatomic particle?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"anion"},{"label":"B","text":"cation"},{"label":"C","text":"molecule"},{"label":"D","text":"neutron"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["source answer/boilerplate removed from option D"]},{"id":"q-9dc2ff6039a3","stem":"Two atoms have the same mass number but different atomic numbers. Which of the following statements concerning these atoms is TRUE?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Each has the same number of neutrons in its nucleus."},{"label":"B","text":"They are isotopes."},{"label":"C","text":"They are atoms of different elements."},{"label":"D","text":"Each has the same number of protons in its nucleus."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"independent_reasoning_unverified_sources","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","t